insert into interview.questions (slug, category_id, formats, difficulty, question_text, station_brief, what_is_being_tested, follow_ups, model_answer_scaffold, model_answer_exemplar, exemplar_annotations, key_points, common_pitfalls, tags, status)
values ($c$jehovahs-witness-refuses-blood$c$, (select id from interview.categories where slug = $c$ethics-professionalism$c$), array[$c$mmi$c$, $c$panel$c$]::interview.interview_format[], 3, $c$A 34-year-old woman who is a Jehovah's Witness has lost a lot of blood after childbirth. She is refusing a blood transfusion, which her doctors believe is necessary to save her life. What should the doctors do?$c$, $c$You are a medical student on an obstetric ward. The patient is alert and has a card in her purse stating she refuses blood products. Her husband is asking the team to 'do whatever it takes'.$c$, $c$Understanding of autonomy and capacity, the right of a capable adult to refuse life-saving treatment, and how to act compassionately within the law.$c$, $c$["What if she were unconscious and had no advance decision?","What if the patient were her 12-year-old daughter?","How should the team respond to her husband?","How would you feel if she died, and how would you cope?"]$c$::jsonb, $c$[{"step":"Identify","guidance":"Tension between respecting a capable adult's refusal (autonomy) and preserving life (beneficence)."},{"step":"Capacity and voluntariness","guidance":"Assess capacity for this decision (understand, retain, weigh, communicate). Check the decision is informed and free from undue pressure — ideally speak to her privately."},{"step":"Information and alternatives","guidance":"Ensure she understands the consequences. Offer every acceptable alternative: cell salvage, IV iron, erythropoietin, surgical control, fluids — and ask which products she will accept (some accept certain fractions)."},{"step":"Law and guidance","guidance":"A capable adult may refuse any treatment, even if it leads to death (Mental Capacity Act principles; GMC guidance). Treating against her will would be assault. Check for an advance decision."},{"step":"Family and staff","guidance":"Explain to her husband compassionately that her decision stands, while supporting him. Involve seniors, document carefully, and support staff."},{"step":"Conclude","guidance":"Respect her informed, capable refusal while providing all other care, including palliative care if needed."}]$c$::jsonb, $c$The central conflict here is between respecting her autonomy and the team's wish to save her life.

The first question is whether she has capacity to make this decision. The Mental Capacity Act says we should start by assuming she does, and consider whether she can understand the information, retain it, weigh it up and communicate a decision. Heavy blood loss could affect her thinking, so this needs careful assessment — but disagreeing with a decision is not evidence that someone lacks capacity. I'd also want the team to speak to her privately, away from family or members of her faith community, to make sure the choice is genuinely hers and not the result of pressure.

If she has capacity and understands that refusing may lead to her death, then the law and GMC guidance are clear: a capable adult can refuse any treatment, even life-saving treatment. Giving blood against her will would be a serious harm and legally an assault.

That doesn't mean the team stops caring. They should explore exactly which products she'd accept, and use every alternative available — cell salvage, iron, drugs to stimulate blood production, surgical control of the bleeding — and involve senior obstetricians, anaesthetists and haematologists early.

Her husband is clearly frightened, and the team should explain gently that the decision is hers, while supporting him. Everything should be documented carefully.

It would be incredibly hard for the team if she died, and they'd need support afterwards. But I think respecting her decision is right, because the alternative is overriding a capable person's deeply held beliefs about her own body.$c$, $c$[{"excerpt":"disagreeing with a decision is not evidence that someone lacks capacity","note":"Key MCA principle: an unwise decision does not mean incapacity."},{"excerpt":"speak to her privately","note":"Checks voluntariness — often missed, and marks out a strong candidate."},{"excerpt":"exactly which products she'd accept","note":"Shows awareness that practice varies between individuals — nuance."},{"excerpt":"It would be incredibly hard for the team","note":"Emotional awareness and staff support — completes a mature answer. Could name the child follow-up distinction for extra depth."}]$c$::jsonb, $c$["Capable adults can refuse life-saving treatment; treating against consent is unlawful.","Assess capacity for the specific decision; don't equate refusal with incapacity.","Check voluntariness — speak to the patient alone.","Offer all acceptable alternatives and involve seniors.","Support family and staff; document.","For children, the courts can authorise treatment in their best interests; in emergencies doctors act to preserve life."]$c$::jsonb, $c$["Saying you would transfuse to save her life.","Assuming she lacks capacity because of her decision.","Letting the husband's wishes override hers.","Treating the four pillars as a list without reaching a decision."]$c$::jsonb, array[$c$autonomy$c$, $c$capacity$c$, $c$refusal-of-treatment$c$, $c$religion$c$]::text[], 'published')
