values ($c$parents-refuse-vaccination$c$, (select id from interview.categories where slug = $c$ethics-professionalism$c$), array[$c$mmi$c$, $c$panel$c$]::interview.interview_format[], 3, $c$Parents of a one-year-old tell the practice nurse they do not want their child to have the MMR vaccine because they have read it can cause autism. How should this be handled?$c$, null, $c$Communication with worried parents, handling misinformation respectfully, children's best interests, and the limits of compulsion.$c$, $c$["Should vaccination be compulsory?","What if one parent wants the vaccine and the other doesn't?","How would you explain herd immunity to these parents?"]$c$::jsonb, $c$[{"step":"Explore concerns","guidance":"ICE: where did they read this, what worries them most, what are they hoping for? Listen without dismissing."},{"step":"Give accurate information","guidance":"The claim linking MMR and autism came from a fraudulent study that was retracted; large studies have found no link. Explain measles risks and herd immunity simply."},{"step":"Respect their role","guidance":"Vaccination in the UK is not compulsory; parents with parental responsibility decide. Avoid coercion; offer time and written information."},{"step":"Best interests and disagreement","guidance":"If parents disagree with each other, it may ultimately need a court decision; the child's best interests are central."},{"step":"Keep the door open","guidance":"Offer follow-up; don't damage the relationship."}]$c$::jsonb, $c$I'd approach this as a conversation rather than a correction, because parents who feel dismissed are more likely to walk away.

I'd start by asking what they've read and what worries them most. It's understandable to be anxious — they're trying to protect their child. Once I understood their concerns, I'd explain honestly that the idea of a link between MMR and autism came from a study published in the 1990s that was later shown to be fraudulent and was retracted, and the doctor involved was struck off. Since then, very large studies in millions of children have found no link.

I'd also explain the other side of the balance: measles can cause serious complications like pneumonia and brain inflammation, and outbreaks have been increasing where vaccine uptake has dropped. Vaccinating their child also helps protect babies too young to be vaccinated and children who can't have vaccines for medical reasons.

But vaccination isn't compulsory in the UK, and the decision is theirs. So I'd give them written information from a reliable source, offer time to think, and invite them back. If they still declined, I'd respect that and make sure they know they can change their mind at any time.

I don't think pressuring them would work or be right — it's about giving them the best chance to make an informed decision for their child.$c$, $c$[{"excerpt":"a conversation rather than a correction","note":"Frames the whole approach around trust."},{"excerpt":"later shown to be fraudulent and was retracted","note":"Accurate, specific rebuttal without mocking the parents."},{"excerpt":"babies too young to be vaccinated","note":"Explains herd immunity in human terms."},{"excerpt":"isn't compulsory in the UK","note":"Recognises limits of compulsion and parental responsibility."}]$c$::jsonb, $c$["Explore concerns first (ICE).","Accurate, respectful correction of misinformation.","Risks of measles and herd immunity.","Parental decision; vaccination is not compulsory in the UK.","Follow-up and an open door."]$c$::jsonb, $c$["Calling the parents irresponsible or stupid.","Overwhelming them with statistics.","Claiming doctors can override parents without a court."]$c$::jsonb, array[$c$vaccination$c$, $c$misinformation$c$, $c$children$c$, $c$communication$c$]::text[], 'published')
