insert into interview.questions (slug, category_id, formats, difficulty, question_text, station_brief, what_is_being_tested, follow_ups, model_answer_scaffold, model_answer_exemplar, exemplar_annotations, key_points, common_pitfalls, tags, status)
values ($c$family-asks-not-to-tell-patient$c$, (select id from interview.categories where slug = $c$ethics-professionalism$c$), array[$c$mmi$c$, $c$panel$c$]::interview.interview_format[], 4, $c$An elderly woman has been diagnosed with advanced cancer. Her son asks the doctor not to tell her, saying the news would 'destroy her'. What should the doctor do?$c$, null, $c$Understanding that information belongs to the patient, balancing honesty with compassion, and handling family requests sensitively.$c$, $c$["Is it ever acceptable not to tell a patient their diagnosis?","What if she had said before that she didn't want to know bad news?","What if the family come from a culture where it's normal not to tell patients?"]$c$::jsonb, $c$[{"step":"Identify","guidance":"The patient's right to information and autonomy versus the family's wish to protect her (their view of her best interests)."},{"step":"Whose information","guidance":"The diagnosis belongs to the patient. A capable adult is entitled to know, and it affects decisions about treatment and her remaining time."},{"step":"Engage the son","guidance":"Explore his fears and knowledge of his mother; acknowledge his love; explain why the doctor can't agree to withhold."},{"step":"Ask the patient","guidance":"Find out how much she wants to know and who she wants involved. Some patients genuinely don't want details — that is their choice, not the family's."},{"step":"How, not whether","guidance":"Break news sensitively using SPIKES; offer support; involve family if she wishes."}]$c$::jsonb, $c$The conflict here is between the son's wish to protect his mother and her right to know about her own health.

I'd start with the son, because he's clearly frightened. I'd want to understand why he thinks the news would destroy her — he knows her far better than the team does, and he might have important insight, like how she's coped with bad news before. I'd acknowledge that he's asking out of love.

But I'd explain gently that the information belongs to his mother. If she has capacity, she has the right to know about her diagnosis, and she may have decisions to make — about treatment, where she wants to be cared for, and things she wants to say or do. Keeping it from her could also damage her trust, and many patients sense that something is wrong, which can make them feel more isolated.

The key is that it isn't the family's decision about how much she's told — it's hers. So the doctor should ask her how much she'd like to know and who she wants with her. Some people genuinely don't want the details, and if she says that, it should be respected.

Then it's about how the news is given — in a quiet setting, at her pace, with support, perhaps with her son present if she wants him there.

So I wouldn't agree to withhold the diagnosis, but I'd work with the son rather than against him, and let his mother guide how much she learns.$c$, $c$[{"excerpt":"he knows her far better than the team does","note":"Treats the family as a source of insight, not an obstacle."},{"excerpt":"the information belongs to his mother","note":"The central principle, stated clearly."},{"excerpt":"Some people genuinely don't want the details","note":"Nuance: the right to know includes the right not to know — decided by the patient."}]$c$::jsonb, $c$["Information belongs to the patient.","Explore and acknowledge the family's concerns.","Ask the patient how much she wants to know.","The right not to know is the patient's, not the family's.","Break news sensitively (SPIKES)."]$c$::jsonb, $c$["Agreeing to withhold the diagnosis because the family asked.","Dismissing the son's concern.","Forcing detailed information on a patient who doesn't want it.","Stereotyping cultural practices."]$c$::jsonb, array[$c$truth-telling$c$, $c$autonomy$c$, $c$family$c$, $c$breaking-bad-news$c$]::text[], 'published')
